  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Friday)\nIssued at 117 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nLONG TERM\n\nLong-term forecast impacts: Potential for a few snowflakes and light \nrain on Tuesday morning in western Oklahoma. Extended period of \ncolder-than-normal temperatures.\n\nDiscussion: A shortwave trough will cross the southern Rockies \nbefore digging for the Gulf Coast on Tuesday. This will place the \ncore of the upper-low and coldest temperatures aloft over our area. \nIt is unlikely that we will see a quick enough low-level response \nfor a warm-advection-driven precip shield to develop. However, the \nsynoptic-scale lift is plausibly enough to bring light precipitation \nto northern and western Oklahoma. Thermal profiles are cold enough \nthat a dusting of snow is possible.\n\nThe trough will be followed by a renewed surge of cold and dry air \nfrom the Upper Midwest. Beginning Wednesday morning, parts of the \narea are likely (30% OKC metro, 90% in northwest Oklahoma) to see \nlows of 28 or below. Hard freezes will bring remaining elements of \ngrowing season to an end and begin the process of curing fine fuels \nfor our winter fire season.\n\nThe most notable aspect of the upcoming cold snap is its length. It \nhas been exceedingly rare so far in 2026 to see us below-normal for \ntemperatures for more than a day or two. Most of the long-term range \n(until Friday or Saturday) will remain colder than normal here, and \nthere are hints of another frontal passage next weekend.\n\nMeister",
